(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027q-027"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027q-027","title":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | Compiler Design | Intermediate Code Generation and Optimization | Weekly Quiz 5","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | Compiler Design | Intermediate Code Generation and Optimization | Weekly Quiz 5","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027q","num":27,"category":"other","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":2700,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"Compiler Design"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027/015_exam_2408_GO Classes GATE CS Compiler Design Intermediate Code Generation and Optimization Weekly Quiz 5.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following high-level code snippet:<br>&nbsp;</p><p>if $(a &lt; b + c)$<br>&nbsp;$a = a - c;$<br>$c = b \\times c;$</p><p>Which of the following represents the correct and valid Three-Address Code (TAC) translation for the given snippet?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$-t1=b+c;$<br>$-t2=a&lt;-t1;$<br>$IFZ\\ -t2\\ Goto\\ L0;$<br>$-t3=a-c;$<br>$a=-t3;$<br>$L0:\\ -t4=b*c;$<br>$c=-t4;$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$-t1=b+c;$<br>$-t2=a&lt;-t1;$<br>$IFNZ\\ -t2\\ Goto\\ L0;$<br>$-t3=a-c;$<br>$a=-t3;$<br>$L0:\\ -t4=b*c;$<br>$c=-t4;$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$-t1=b+c;$<br>$-t2=a&lt;-t1;$<br>$IFZ\\ -t2\\ Goto\\ L0;$<br>$-t3=a-c;$<br>$a=-t3;$<br>$-t4=b*c;$<br>$c=-t4;$<br>$L0:$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\_t1 = a &lt; b + c;$<br>IfZ $\\_t1$ Goto $\\_L0;$<br>$\\_t2 = a - c;$<br>$a = \\_t2;$<br>$\\_L0:$ $\\_t3 = b \\times c;$<br>$c = \\_t3;$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532388\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Lets systematically convert the high-level code into Three-Address Code (TAC).<br>\n<br>\nA key rule of TAC is that each instruction can have at most one operator on the right-hand side.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{Step\\ 1:\\ Evaluate\\ the\\ conditional\\ expression\\ } (a&lt;b+c)$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ We cannot evaluate $a&lt;b+c$ in a single step because it contains two operations $(+ \\text{ and } &lt;)$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ First, we compute the addition:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n-t1=b+c;<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ Next, we evaluate the relational operator:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n-t2=a&lt;-t1;<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{Step\\ 2:\\ Handle\\ the\\ control\\ flow\\ } (\\text{the if statement})$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ If the condition is false (i.e., zero), the program should skip the if block.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ We use an $IFZ$ jump to a label $(L0)$ placed immediately after the if block:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIFZ\\ -t2\\ Goto\\ L0;<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{Step\\ 3:\\ Translate\\ the\\ if\\ block\\ } (a=a-c;)$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ Compute the subtraction into a temporary variable:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n-t3=a-c;<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ Assign it to $a$:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\na=-t3;<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{Step\\ 4:\\ Translate\\ the\\ code\\ following\\ the\\ if\\ block}$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ The target label $L0$ is placed here.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ Compute the multiplication:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n-t4=b*c;<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ Assign it to $c$:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nc=-t4;<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nCombining these steps yields the exact sequence found in Option A.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{Option\\ B:}$ Uses $IFNZ$ (If Not Zero). This would incorrectly skip the if body when the condition is true.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{Option\\ C:}$ Places the label $L0$ at the very end of the snippet. If the condition is false, it skips both the if body and the subsequent $(c=b*c;)$ assignment, altering the program's logic.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{Option\\ D:}$ Violates the fundamental structure of TAC in its first line $(-t1=a&lt;b+c;)$, which contains two operators on the right-hand side.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"534007\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">If Zero flag is set then execute c=b x c else go with normal flow</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532387\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":2,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>In the context of compiler design, an Intermediate Representation ($IR$) acts as a bridge between the source language and the target machine architecture. Based on the fundamental principles of compiler architecture, which of the following statements correctly describe the advantages and implications of using a common Intermediate Representation? (Select all that apply)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Portability is significantly improved because adding support for a new target machine architecture only requires developing a new back-end, leaving the existing front-ends completely unchanged.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>In a compiler ecosystem designed to support $M$ different source languages and $N$ different target architectures, utilizing a universal IR reduces the total number of required translation modules (front-ends and back-ends) from $M\\times N$ to $M+N$.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>To achieve maximum execution efficiency, machine-independent code optimization passes must be independently rewritten for every distinct source language supported by the compiler.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The intermediate representation can serve as a directly interpretable format, allowing the program to be executed in a virtual machine environment without requiring full compilation to native machine code.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532386\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Lets analyze each option based on the architectural benefits of using an Intermediate Representation (IR):<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{A\\ is\\ CORRECT:}$ The primary purpose of dividing a compiler into a front-end and a back-end via an IR is portability. The front-end handles language-specific details (translating source $\\to$ IR), and the back-end handles machine-specific details (translating IR $\\to$ target code). To support a new architecture, you only write a back-end that translates the IR to that new machine code.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{B\\ is\\ CORRECT:}$ Without an IR, a monolithic compiler must be written for every language-to-architecture pair, resulting in $M\\times N$ distinct compilers (e.g., C to x86, C to ARM, Java to x86, Java to ARM). With a common IR, you only need $M$ front-ends (Source $\\to$ IR) and $N$ back-ends (IR $\\to$ Target), reducing the engineering effort to $M+N$ modules.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{C\\ is\\ INCORRECT:}$ One of the major advantages of an IR is exactly the opposite of this statement. Because the IR is independent of both the source language and the target machine, optimization algorithms (like dead code elimination or constant folding) only need to be written once for the IR, rather than being rewritten for every source language.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{D\\ is\\ CORRECT:}$ An intermediate representation is not purely conceptual; it can be structurally executed. A classic example is Java Bytecode, which acts as an IR that is directly interpreted by the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532385\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":3,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following sequence of $\\text{Three-Address Code (TAC)}$ generated by a compiler for a high-level repeat-until loop construct:</p><div style=\"width:fit-content; margin:0 auto;\"><pre>1   A = 4\n2   t1 = A * B\n3   L1: t2 = t1 / C\n4   if t2 &lt; W goto L2\n5   M = t1 * k\n6   t3 = M + I\n7   L2: H = I\n8   M = t3 - H\n9   if t3 &gt;= 0 goto L3\n10  goto L1\n11  L3: halt\n</pre></div><p>A Control Flow Graph ($CFG$) is constructed from this $TAC$ using standard leader-finding algorithms. How many strictly defined $\\textbf{Basic Blocks}$ are formed in this $CFG$?</p><p><em>(Note: Exclude any dummy ENTRY and EXIT blocks from your count.)</em></p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532378\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"500\" height=\"557\" src=\"media/6b/6b90e6760b9f4ca70db2.png\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"534010\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Question is not clear in the test interface. Pls correct it <span class=\"pupi_dm_user_mention\" data-pupi_dm_user_id=\"181161\"><a class=\"pupi_dm_user_mention\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/user/GO%20Classes\" target=\"_blank\">@Dvruo Classes</a></span></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532377\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":4,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Consider the following high-level code snippet:<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\n&amp;x:=0; \\\\<br>\n&amp;y:=0; \\\\<br>\n&amp;while\\ (n&gt;0)\\ \\{ \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\qquad if\\ (n\\%2==0)\\ \\{ \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\qquad\\qquad x:=x+n; \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\qquad\\qquad y:=y+1; \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\qquad \\} \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\qquad else\\ \\{ \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\qquad\\qquad y:=y+n; \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\qquad\\qquad x:=x+1; \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\qquad \\} \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\qquad n:=n-1; \\\\<br>\n&amp;\\} \\\\<br>\n&amp;print(x);<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nWhen this code is translated into standard Three-Address Code (TAC) with explicit jump instructions for the control flow structures (while and if-else), the compiler groups the instructions into Basic Blocks.<br>\n<br>\nUsing the standard algorithm for partitioning three-address code, what is the total number of $\\textbf{Leaders}$ identified in the intermediate code?","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532376\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"550\" height=\"402\" src=\"media/0f/0ff84fae8f661ba77b37.jpg\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532375\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>During the compilation process, the compiler performs various transformations across different phases. Consider the following list of compiler design techniques:<br><br>$1$. Common Subexpression Elimination<br><br>$2$. Left Factoring<br><br>$3$. Register Allocation<br><br>$4$. Constant Folding<br><br>$5$. Peephole Optimization<br><br>$6$. Strength Reduction<br><br>Which of the techniques listed above specifically belong to the machineindependent intermediate code optimization phase?<br>Options:</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1, 4$, and $6$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$3, 4$, and $5$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$2, 4$, and&nbsp;$5$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1,2,5$, and $6$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532372\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Lets analyze each of the listed techniques based on where they fit into the standard phases of a compiler:<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{1.\\ Common\\ Subexpression\\ Elimination:\\ Intermediate\\ Code\\ Optimization.}$ This is a machine-independent technique that identifies and eliminates redundant calculations by reusing previously computed values.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{2.\\ Left\\ Factoring:\\ Syntax\\ Analysis.}$ This is a grammar transformation technique used early in the compilation process to remove non-determinism, making a grammar suitable for top-down predictive parsers. It is not an optimization technique.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{3.\\ Register\\ Allocation:\\ Target\\ Code\\ Generation.}$ This is a highly machine-dependent phase that maps intermediate variables to the limited physical registers of a specific CPU architecture.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{4.\\ Constant\\ Folding:\\ Intermediate\\ Code\\ Optimization.}$ This machine-independent technique evaluates constant expressions at compile time (e.g., transforming $3\\times4$ into $12$) to save execution time at runtime.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{5.\\ Peephole\\ Optimization:\\ Target\\ Code\\ Optimization.}$ This is a machine-dependent technique that examines a short, sliding window (the peephole) of target machine instructions and replaces them with a faster or shorter sequence (e.g., replacing a load and store to the same location).<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{6.\\ Strength\\ Reduction:\\ Intermediate\\ Code\\ Optimization.}$ This machine-independent technique replaces computationally expensive operations with cheaper equivalents (e.g., replacing $x\\times2$ with $x+x$, or using bit shifts).<br>\n<br>\nTherefore, the only techniques that belong to the machine-independent intermediate code optimization phase are $\\textbf{1,\\ 4,\\ and\\ 6}$.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532371\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":6,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the arithmetic expression $a+-(b+c)$. The corresponding syntax tree for this expression is provided below:<br>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"250\" height=\"277\" src=\"media/73/7303f390c286bcf9ad48.png\"></p><p><br><br>You are tasked with analyzing this syntax tree and the translation of the expression into quadruples. Which of the following statements are $\\textbf{CORRECT}$? (Select all that apply)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>In the provided syntax tree, the internal nodes correctly represent the operators, and the leaf nodes represent the operands (variables $a, b$, and $c$).<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>When representing this expression using standard quadruples, the instruction responsible for the unary minus operator ($\\mathrm{u\\ minus}$) will only utilize the $\\mathrm{op}$, $\\mathrm{arg}_1$, and $\\mathrm{result}$ fields, leaving the $\\mathrm{arg}_2$ field empty (or null).<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>A post-order traversal of the provided syntax tree yields the postfix expression (Reverse Polish Notation): $\\mathrm{a} \\mathrm{b}+\\mathrm{c}$ uminus +.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>A standard translation into quadruples (without applying any aggressive register allocation or optimization) requires a minimum of four distinct temporary variables to evaluate the entire tree.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532392\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Lets break down the properties of the provided syntax tree and the translation of the expression $a+-(b+c)$ into quadruples to evaluate each option.<br><br>$\\textbf{1.\\ Syntax\\ Tree\\ Analysis:}$<br><br>As shown in the question, the syntax tree correctly captures the precedence and associativity of the expression:<br>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"250\" height=\"277\" src=\"media/73/7303f390c286bcf9ad48.png\"></p><p><br><br><br>$\\textbf{2.\\ Quadruple\\ Generation:}$<br><br>Quadruples consist of four fields: $op,\\ arg_1,\\ arg_2,\\ result$. Traversing the syntax tree $\\{type:\\ plusop\\}$ generates the following standard intermediate code:<br><br>$1.$ $(+,\\ b,\\ c,\\ t_1)$ // evaluates the inner $(b+c)$<br><br>$2.$ $(uminus,\\ t_1,\\ -,\\ t_2)$ // applies unary minus to the result<br><br>$3.$ $(+,\\ a,\\ t_2,\\ t_3)$ // adds $a$ to the negated result<br><br>Evaluating the Options:<br><br>$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{A\\ is\\ CORRECT:}$ Looking at the tree, the internal nodes $+,\\ uminus,\\ +$ are indeed all operators, and the leaf nodes $a,\\ b,\\ c$ are the operands. This is the definition of a syntax tree.<br><br>$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{B\\ is\\ CORRECT:}$ Unary operators operate on a single operand. In the quadruple representation, step $2$ clearly shows that the $\\text{arg}_2$ field remains unused (often represented with a dash, empty space, or null) for unary operations like $uminus$.<br><br>$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{C\\ is\\ INCORRECT:}$ A post-order traversal (Left child, Right child, Root) of the syntax tree evaluates as follows:<br><br>$\\bullet$ Traverse left child of root: $a$<br><br>$\\bullet$ Traverse right child of root $(uminus$ subtree$):$<br><br>$\\bullet$ Traverse left child of $uminus$: $b$<br><br>$\\bullet$ Traverse right child of $+$: $c$<br><br>$\\bullet$ Visit inner $+$<br><br>$\\bullet$ Visit $uminus$<br><br>$\\bullet$ Visit root $+$<br><br>The correct postfix string is $abc+uminus+$. The string given in option $C$ $(a+b+c\\ uminus+)$ is incorrect.<br><br>$\\bullet$ $\\textbf{D\\ is\\ INCORRECT:}$ As demonstrated in the quadruple generation steps above, evaluating the entire tree requires exactly three temporary variables $(t_1,\\ t_2,\\ t_3)$, not four.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532391\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following assignment statements commonly analyzed during intermediate code generation:<br><br>$\\textbf{I.}$ $a[i]=b * c-b * d$<br><br>$\\textbf{II.}$ $x=f(y+1)+2$<br><br>Match the target intermediate code formats in List $\\textbf{I}$ with their corresponding correct sequence of instructions in List $\\textbf{II}$.<br><br>$$<br>\\begin{array}{|l|l|}<br>\\hline<br>\\textbf{List I (Target Format)} &amp; \\textbf{List II (Instruction Sequence)} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\textbf{P.} \\text{Triples representation for statement I} &amp;<br>1. \\\\<br>\\texttt{a[i] = b*c - b*d} &amp;<br>\\begin{aligned}<br>(0)\\;&amp; + \\qquad i \\qquad 1 \\\\<br>(1)\\;&amp; \\text{param} \\qquad (0) \\\\<br>(2)\\;&amp; \\text{call} \\qquad f \\qquad 1 \\\\<br>(3)\\;&amp; + \\qquad (2) \\qquad 2 \\\\<br>(4)\\;&amp; - \\qquad x \\qquad (3)<br>\\end{aligned}<br>\\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\textbf{Q.} \\text{Triples representation for statement II} &amp;<br>2. \\text{Instruction Array + Triples Array} \\\\<br>\\texttt{x = f(y+1) + 2} &amp;<br>\\begin{aligned}<br>(11)\\;&amp; \\rightarrow (0) \\qquad + \\qquad y \\qquad 1 \\\\<br>(12)\\;&amp; \\rightarrow (1) \\qquad \\text{param} \\qquad (0) \\\\<br>(13)\\;&amp; \\rightarrow (2) \\qquad \\text{call} \\qquad f \\qquad 1 \\\\<br>(14)\\;&amp; \\rightarrow (3) \\qquad + \\qquad (2) \\qquad 2 \\\\<br>(15)\\;&amp; \\rightarrow (4) \\qquad = \\qquad x \\qquad (3)<br>\\end{aligned}<br>\\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\textbf{R.} \\text{Indirect Triples representation} &amp;<br>3. \\\\<br>\\text{for statement II} &amp;<br>\\begin{aligned}<br>(0)\\;&amp; * \\qquad b \\qquad c \\\\<br>(1)\\;&amp; * \\qquad b \\qquad d \\\\<br>(2)\\;&amp; - \\qquad (0) \\qquad (1) \\\\<br>(3)\\;&amp; []= \\qquad a \\qquad i \\\\<br>(4)\\;&amp; - \\qquad (3) \\qquad (2)<br>\\end{aligned}<br>\\\\<br>\\texttt{x = f(y+1) + 2}<br>&amp;<br>\\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\end{array}<br>$$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$P - 3,\\; Q - 1,\\; R - 2$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$P - 1,\\; Q - 3,\\; R - 2$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$P - 3,\\; Q - 2,\\; R - 1$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$P - 2,\\; Q - 1,\\; R - 3$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532390\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Let's break down the intermediate code generation for both statements:<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{1.\\ Statement\\ I:}\\ a[i]=bc*de$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ Triples restrict us to a maximum of three fields (operator, operand1, operand2). Temporary variables are implicitly represented by the row index of the operation that computed them.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ We first compute $bc$ at index $(0)$ and $de$ at index $(1)$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ We abstract the result of $(1)$ from $(0)$ at index $(2)$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ Array assignment are typically represented using two instructions in standard triples: one to resolve the array reference $(i,\\ a,\\ i)$ at index $(3)$, and another to assign the value to that resolved reference - $(=,\\ (3),\\ (2))$ at index $(4)$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ This sequence precisely aligns with Representation $3$. Thus, $P\\to 3$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{2.\\ Statement\\ II:}\\ x=f(y+1)+2$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ The inner expression $y+1$ evaluates first at $(0)$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ The value from $(0)$ is passed as a parameter using the $param$ operator at $(1)$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ The function $f$ is invoked with $1$ argument using the $call$ operator at $(2)$. The result of the call is implicitly stored at $(2)$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ We add $2$ to the function's result at $(3)$ - $(2),\\ 2$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ Finally, we assign the result of $(3)$ to $x$ at $(4)$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ This standard triples format matches Representation $1$. Thus, $Q\\to 1$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{3.\\ Indirect\\ Triples}$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ Indirect Triples use the exact same underlying triples array. However, instead of referencing the triples directly by their memory address during execution, a separate Instruction Array of pointers (e.g., locations $11,\\ 12,\\ 13,\\ldots$) is maintained. Each pointer links to a specific triple $(0,\\ 1,\\ 2,\\ldots)$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ This separation allows the compilers optimizer to rewrite instructions by simply moving the pointers in the Instruction Array, without altering the hardcoded index references within the Triples array itself.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet$ This structural difference matches Representation $2$. Thus, $R\\to 2$.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532389\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following original code segment:</p><p>\\[ \\begin{array}{l} x = 5 \\\\ x = x - 3 \\\\ \\text{if } x &lt; 3 \\\\ \\quad y = x * 2 \\\\ \\quad w = y \\\\ \\text{else} \\\\ \\quad y = x - 3 \\\\ w = x - y \\\\ z = x + y \\end{array} \\]</p><p>Which of the following represents the correct translation of the original code into SSA form?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<pre><div class=\"bijzli-math-block\">\\[\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\texttt{x\\_1 = 5}\\\\\n\\texttt{x\\_2 = x\\_1 - 3}\\\\\n\\texttt{if x\\_2 &lt; 3}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{y\\_1 = x\\_2 * 2}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{w\\_1 = y\\_1}\\\\\n\\texttt{else}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{y\\_1 = x\\_2 - 3}\\\\\n\\texttt{w\\_2 = x\\_2 - y\\_1}\\\\\n\\texttt{z\\_1 = x\\_2 + y\\_1}\n\\end{array}\n\\]</div></pre>"},{"key":"B","html":"<pre><div class=\"bijzli-math-block\">\\[\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\texttt{x\\_1 = 5}\\\\\n\\texttt{x\\_2 = x\\_1 - 3}\\\\\n\\texttt{if x\\_2 &lt; 3}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{y\\_1 = x\\_2 * 2}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{w\\_1 = y\\_1}\\\\\n\\texttt{else}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{y\\_2 = x\\_2 - 3}\\\\\n\\texttt{w\\_2 = x\\_2 - y\\_1}\\\\\n\\texttt{z\\_1 = x\\_2 + y\\_2}\n\\end{array}\n\\]</div></pre>"},{"key":"C","html":"<pre><div class=\"bijzli-math-block\">\\[\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\texttt{x\\_1 = 5}\\\\\n\\texttt{x\\_2 = x\\_1 - 3}\\\\\n\\texttt{if x\\_2 &lt; 3}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{y\\_1 = x\\_2 * 2}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{w\\_1 = y\\_1}\\\\\n\\texttt{else}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{y\\_2 = x\\_2 - 3}\\\\\n\\texttt{y\\_3 = }\\Phi\\texttt{(y\\_1, y\\_2)}\\\\\n\\texttt{w\\_2 = x\\_2 - y\\_3}\\\\\n\\texttt{z\\_1 = x\\_2 + y\\_3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]</div></pre>"},{"key":"D","html":"<pre><div class=\"bijzli-math-block\">\\[\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\texttt{x\\_1 = 5}\\\\\n\\texttt{x\\_2 = x\\_1 - 3}\\\\\n\\texttt{if x\\_2 &lt; 3}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{y\\_1 = x\\_2 * 2}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{w\\_1 = y\\_1}\\\\\n\\texttt{else}\\\\\n\\qquad \\texttt{y\\_2 = x\\_2 - 3}\\\\\n\\texttt{w\\_2 = }\\Phi\\texttt{(w\\_1, 0)}\\\\\n\\texttt{y\\_3 = }\\Phi\\texttt{(y\\_1, y\\_2)}\\\\\n\\texttt{w\\_3 = x\\_2 - y\\_3}\\\\\n\\texttt{z\\_1 = x\\_2 + y\\_3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]</div></pre>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532384\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>$\\textbf{Converting to SSA}$</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"250\" height=\"167\" src=\"media/70/70642357249102bd5cc7.jpg\"></p><p><br><br>$\\Phi(y_1, y_2)$ is defined as $y_1$ if we arrive at this instruction through the THEN branch, $y_2$ if through the ELSE branch.</p><p>One needs to introduce $\\Phi$ functions at every point of the program where several \"paths'' merge.<br>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"533235\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><span class=\"pupi_dm_user_mention\" data-pupi_dm_user_id=\"181161\"><a class=\"pupi_dm_user_mention\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/user/GO%20Classes\" target=\"_blank\">@Dvruo Classes</a>..</span>In <strong>C option,w2 is not there...Then,How C is right answer..?..Best option is D..It is correct and there is spell mistake y3 instead of w2</strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532383\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following high-level code segment:</p><div style=\"width:fit-content; margin:0 auto;\"><pre>for i from 1 to 10 do\n            for j from 1 to 10 do\n                        a[i, j] = 0.0;\nfor i from 1 to 10 do\n            a[i, i] = 1.0;\n</pre></div><p>When this code is translated into intermediate \\(\\text{Three-Address Code (TAC)}\\) and subsequently partitioned into a $\\text{Control Flow Graph (CFG)}$ how many standard basic blocks are formed?</p><p><em>(Note: Exclude the dummy $ENTRY$ and $EXIT$ blocks from your final count.)</em></p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532382\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"500\" height=\"752\" src=\"media/b4/b4f2fcd79661ad44486d.png\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532381\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following high-level code snippet containing a conditional statement within a repeat-until loop:<br>$$<br>\\begin{aligned}<br>&amp;A=4 \\\\<br>&amp;t1=A*B \\\\<br>&amp;repeat\\ \\{ \\\\<br>&amp;\\qquad t2=t1/C \\\\<br>&amp;\\qquad if\\ (t2&gt;=W)\\ \\{ \\\\<br>&amp;\\qquad\\qquad M=t1*k \\\\<br>&amp;\\qquad\\qquad t3=M+I \\\\<br>&amp;\\qquad \\} \\\\<br>&amp;\\qquad H=I \\\\<br>&amp;\\qquad M=t3-H \\\\<br>&amp;\\}\\ until\\ (t3&gt;=0)<br>\\end{aligned}<br>$$<br>Which of the following represents the correct and valid translation of this code into Three-Address Code (TAC) using standard short-circuit conditional branching?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1\\qquad A=4$</p><p>$2\\qquad t1=A*B$</p><p>$3\\qquad L1:\\ t2=t1/C$</p><p>$4\\qquad if\\ t2&lt;W\\ goto\\ L2$</p><p>$5\\qquad M=t1*k$</p><p>$6\\qquad t3=M+I$</p><p>$7\\qquad L2:\\ H=I$</p><p>$8\\qquad M=t3-H$</p><p>$9\\qquad if\\ t3&gt;=0\\ goto\\ L3$</p><p>$10\\qquad goto\\ L1$</p><p>$11\\qquad L3:\\ halt$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$1\\qquad A=4$<br><br>$2\\qquad t1=A*B$<br><br>$3\\qquad L1:\\ t2=t1/C$<br><br>$4\\qquad if\\ t2&gt;=W\\ goto\\ L2$<br><br>$5\\qquad M=t1*k$<br><br>$6\\qquad t3=M+I$<br><br>$7\\qquad L2:\\ H=I$<br><br>$8\\qquad M=t3-H$<br><br>$9\\qquad if\\ t3&lt;0\\ goto\\ L1$<br><br>$10\\qquad L3:\\ halt$<br><br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1\\qquad A=4$<br><br>$2\\qquad t1=A*B$<br><br>$3\\qquad L1:\\ t2=t1/C$<br><br>$4\\qquad if\\ t2&lt;W\\ goto\\ L2$<br><br>$5\\qquad M=t1*k$<br><br>$6\\qquad t3=M+I$<br><br>$7\\qquad L2:\\ H=I$<br><br>$8\\qquad M=t3-H$<br><br>$9\\qquad if\\ t3&gt;=0\\ goto\\ L1$<br><br>$10\\qquad halt$<br><br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1\\qquad A=4$<br><br>$2\\qquad t1=A*B$<br><br>$3\\qquad L1:\\ t2=t1/C$<br><br>$4\\qquad if\\ t2&lt;W\\ goto\\ L2$<br><br>$5\\qquad M=t1*k$<br><br>$6\\qquad t3=M+I$<br><br>$7\\qquad L2:\\ H=I$<br><br>$8\\qquad M=t3-H$<br><br>$9\\qquad if\\ t3&lt;0\\ goto\\ L3$<br><br>$10\\qquad goto\\ L1$<br><br>$11\\qquad L3:\\ halt$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532380\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">$\\textbf{Running\\ example}$<br>\n<br>\n$1\\qquad A=4$<br>\n<br>\n$2\\qquad t1=A*B$<br>\n<br>\n$3\\qquad L1:\\ t2=t1/C$<br>\n<br>\n$4\\qquad if\\ t2&lt;W\\ goto\\ L2$<br>\n<br>\n$5\\qquad M=t1*k$<br>\n<br>\n$6\\qquad t3=M+I$<br>\n<br>\n$7\\qquad L2:\\ H=I$<br>\n<br>\n$8\\qquad M=t3-H$<br>\n<br>\n$9\\qquad if\\ t3&gt;=0\\ goto\\ L3$<br>\n<br>\n$10\\qquad goto\\ L1$<br>\n<br>\n$11\\qquad L3:\\ halt$</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532379\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":11,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following assignment statement involving arithmetic expressions:<br>$$ a=b*-c+b*-c $$ Assuming a standard, unoptimized intermediate code generation process where unary minus binds tighter than multiplication and is treated as a distinct operator (uminus), which of the following intermediate representations CORRECTLY model the unoptimized evaluation of this expression? (Select all that apply)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p><strong>Three-Address Code</strong></p>$$ \\begin{aligned} t_1 &amp;= \\text{minus } c \\\\ t_2 &amp;= b * t_1 \\\\ t_3 &amp;= \\text{minus } c \\\\ t_4 &amp;= b * t_3 \\\\ t_5 &amp;= t_2 + t_4 \\\\ a &amp;= t_5 \\end{aligned} $$"},{"key":"B","html":"<p><strong>Quadruples</strong></p>$$ \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|} \\hline &amp; op &amp; arg1 &amp; arg2 &amp; result \\\\ \\hline 0 &amp; \\text{minus} &amp; c &amp; &amp; t_1 \\\\ 1 &amp; * &amp; b &amp; t_1 &amp; t_2 \\\\ 2 &amp; \\text{minus} &amp; c &amp; &amp; t_3 \\\\ 3 &amp; * &amp; b &amp; t_3 &amp; t_4 \\\\ 4 &amp; + &amp; t_2 &amp; t_4 &amp; t_5 \\\\ 5 &amp; = &amp; t_5 &amp; &amp; a \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} $$"},{"key":"C","html":"<p><strong>Syntax Tree</strong><br>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"260\" src=\"media/b4/b4f2c9d91cd2e1973fc3.png\"></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p><strong>Triples</strong></p>$$ \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|} \\hline &amp; op &amp; arg1 &amp; arg2 \\\\ \\hline 0 &amp; * &amp; b &amp; c \\\\ 1 &amp; \\text{minus} &amp; (0) &amp; \\\\ 2 &amp; * &amp; b &amp; c \\\\ 3 &amp; \\text{minus} &amp; (2) &amp; \\\\ 4 &amp; + &amp; (1) &amp; (3) \\\\ 5 &amp; = &amp; a &amp; (4) \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} $$"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532374\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>$\\textbf{Data\\ structures\\ for\\ TAC}$<br><br>$\\textbf{Quadruples}$<br><br>Three-address code for the assignment<br><br>$$<br>a=b*-c+b*-c;<br>$$<br>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"500\" height=\"284\" src=\"media/87/8742d96239eb7c1191b0.jpg\"></p><p><br>$\\textbf{Data\\ structures\\ for\\ TAC}$<br><br>$\\textbf{Quadruples}$<br><br>Three-address code for the assignment<br><br>$$<br>a=b*-c+b*-c;<br>$$</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"500\" height=\"335\" src=\"media/de/dec5e400364199982bfe.jpg\"></p><p><br>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532373\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":12,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following intermediate code segment for which Liveness Analysis (Live Variable Analysis) needs to be performed.<br><br>\\[<br>\\begin{aligned}<br>&amp;e=1; \\\\<br>&amp;L1:\\ if\\ (x\\leq0)\\ goto\\ L\\_END; \\\\<br>&amp;z=e*e; \\\\<br>&amp;y=e*x; \\\\<br>&amp;x=x-1; \\\\<br>&amp;if\\ (x\\ \\&amp;\\ 1)\\ goto\\ L2; \\\\<br>&amp;e=y; \\\\<br>&amp;goto\\ L1; \\\\<br>&amp;L2:\\ e=z; \\\\<br>&amp;goto\\ L1; \\\\<br>&amp;L\\_END: \\\\<br>&amp;\\qquad return\\ x;<br>\\end{aligned}<br>\\]<br>First, the code is partitioned into proper Basic Blocks $(B_1\\ to\\ B_6)$ according to standard compiler design algorithms, ending each block at a branch or jump. Let $B_3$ be the block containing the core loop body instructions:<br>$$<br>z=e*e;\\ y=e*x;\\ x=x-1;\\ if\\ (x\\neq1)<br>$$<br>After performing Live Variable Analysis until convergence, what is the set of live variables at the end of Basic Block $B_3$ (i.e., $OUT[B_3]$)?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\{e,x\\}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>&nbsp;$\\{x,y,z\\}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>&nbsp;$\\{e,x,z\\}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\{x,y\\}$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532370\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_1:\\ e=1;$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_2:\\ if\\ (x&gt;0)\\ \\ (Loop\\ Header)$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_3:\\ z=e*e;\\ y=e*x;\\ x=x-1;\\ if\\ (x\\ \\&amp;\\ 1)$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_4:\\ return\\ x;\\ \\ (Exit\\ Block)$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_5:\\ e=z;\\ goto\\ B_2\\ \\ (True\\ branch\\ of\\ x\\ \\&amp;\\ 1)$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_6:\\ e=y;\\ goto\\ B_2\\ \\ (False\\ branch\\ of\\ x\\ \\&amp;\\ 1)$<br>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"500\" height=\"367\" src=\"media/03/039e72ac68fc3bc48649.jpg\"></p><p><br><br>$\\textbf{2.\\ Liveness\\ Analysis\\ Formulas}$<br><br>Liveness Analysis is a $\\textbf{backward}$ data-flow analysis. The data-flow equations for a basic block $B$ are:<br><br>$$<br>IN[B]=USE[B]\\cup(OUT[B]-DEF[B])<br>\\tag{1}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B]=\\bigcup_{S\\in succ(B)}IN[S]<br>\\tag{2}<br>$$<br><br>Where:<br><br>$\\textbf{Iteration\\ 1}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_6:\\ OUT=IN[B_2]=\\emptyset,\\qquad IN=\\{y\\}\\cup(\\emptyset-\\{e\\})=\\{y\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_5:\\ OUT=IN[B_2]=\\emptyset,\\qquad IN=\\{z\\}\\cup(\\emptyset-\\{e\\})=\\{z\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_4:\\ OUT=\\emptyset\\ (exit),\\qquad IN=\\{x\\}\\cup(\\emptyset-\\emptyset)=\\{x\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_3:\\ OUT=IN[B_5]\\cup IN[B_6]=\\{z,y\\},$<br><br>$\\qquad IN=\\{e,x\\}\\cup(\\{y,z\\}-\\{x,y,z\\})=\\{e,x\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_2:\\ OUT=IN[B_3]\\cup IN[B_4]=\\{e,x\\}\\cup\\{x\\}=\\{e,x\\},$<br><br>$\\qquad IN=\\{x\\}\\cup(\\{e,x\\}-\\emptyset)=\\{e,x\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_1:\\ OUT=IN[B_2]=\\{e,x\\},\\qquad IN=\\emptyset\\cup(\\{e,x\\}-\\{e\\})=\\{x\\}$<br><br>$\\textbf{Iteration\\ 2}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_6:\\ OUT=IN[B_2]=\\{e,x\\},\\qquad IN=\\{y\\}\\cup(\\{e,x\\}-\\{e\\})=\\{x,y\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_5:\\ OUT=IN[B_2]=\\{e,x\\},\\qquad IN=\\{z\\}\\cup(\\{e,x\\}-\\{e\\})=\\{x,z\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_4:\\ OUT=\\emptyset,\\qquad IN=\\{x\\}\\ (unchanged)$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_3:\\ OUT=IN[B_5]\\cup IN[B_6]=\\{x,z\\}\\cup\\{x,y\\}=\\{x,y,z\\},$<br><br>$\\qquad IN=\\{e,x\\}\\cup(\\{x,y,z\\}-\\{x,y,z\\})=\\{e,x\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_2:\\ OUT=IN[B_3]\\cup IN[B_4]=\\{e,x\\},\\qquad IN=\\{x\\}\\cup\\{e,x\\}=\\{e,x\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_1:\\ OUT=\\{e,x\\},\\qquad IN=\\{x\\}$<br><br>$\\textbf{Iteration\\ 3\\ (Convergence)}$<br><br>Re-evaluating $B_6,\\ B_5,\\ B_3$ yields no new variables. For example, in $B_3$,<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_3]=IN[B_5]\\cup IN[B_6]=\\{x,z\\}\\cup\\{x,y\\}=\\{x,y,z\\}.<br>$$<br><br>Since the sets stop changing, the algorithm has converged.<br><br>$\\textbf{Conclusion:}$ As computed during convergence, the set of live variables at the end of Basic Block $3\\ (OUT[B_3])$ is exactly $\\{x,y,z\\}$.<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ USE[B]:$ Variables whose values are read in $B$ before any assignment in $B$.<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ DEF[B]:$ Variables assigned values in $B$.<br><br>$\\textbf{3.\\ Local\\ Sets\\ }(USE\\ and\\ DEF)\\ \\textbf{per\\ Block}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_1:\\ USE=\\emptyset,\\ DEF=\\{e\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_2:\\ USE=\\{x\\},\\ DEF=\\emptyset$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_3:$ Uses $e,\\ x$ before defining them. Defines $z,\\ y,\\ x$.<br><br>$\\qquad USE=\\{e,x\\},\\ DEF=\\{z,y,x\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_4:\\ USE=\\{x\\},\\ DEF=\\emptyset$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_5:\\ USE=\\{z\\},\\ DEF=\\{e\\}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_6:\\ USE=\\{y\\},\\ DEF=\\{e\\}$<br><br>$\\textbf{4.\\ Iterations\\ to\\ Convergence}$<br><br>We initialize all $IN$ and $OUT$ sets to $\\emptyset$. We iterate backwards (from $B_6$ up to $B_1$) until no sets change.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532369\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":13,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following Control Flow Graph (CFG) where each node represents a basic block containing a single instruction or control flow operation. We want to perform an Available Expressions Analysis to identify common subexpressions for redundancy elimination.<br>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"500\" height=\"558\" src=\"media/4c/4c88bd64c1c087923773.png\"></p><p><br><br>Based on the rigorous computation of $IN$ and $OUT$ sets for the Available Expressions Analysis, which of the following statements are $\\textbf{CORRECT?}$ (Select all that apply)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>In block $7$, the expression $a+b$ is a redundant common subexpression.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>In block $8$, the expression $d+e$ is a redundant common subexpression.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>In block $9$, the expression $g+h$ is a redundant common subexpression.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>At the entry of block $7$ (i.e., $IN[7]$), the set of strictly available expressions is exactly $\\{a+b,x*2\\}$.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532368\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Available Expressions Analysis is a $\\textbf{forward}$ data-flow analysis used to find redundant expressions. An expression $E$ evaluated in block $N$ is considered redundant if $E$ is already available at the entry of $N$ (i.e., $E\\in IN[N]$).<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{1.\\ Formal\\ Data\\text{-}Flow\\ Equations}$<br>\n<br>\nLet $U$ be the universal set of expressions appearing on the right-hand side in the program:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nU=\\{a+b,\\ d+e,\\ g+h,\\ x*2\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nLocal Sets for each block $N$:<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Eval[N]\\ (or\\ Gen[N]):$ Set of expressions generated (evaluated) in $N$ and not subsequently killed in $N$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Kill[N]:$ Set of expressions in $U$ that are killed in $N$. An expression is killed if any of its operand variables are assigned a new value in $N$.<br>\n<br>\nGlobal Equations:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[N]=\\bigcap_{P\\in pred(N)}OUT[P]<br>\n\\qquad<br>\n(For\\ N\\neq entry.\\ If\\ N=entry,\\ IN[N]=\\emptyset)<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[N]=Eval[N]\\cup(IN[N]-Kill[N])<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{2.\\ Initialization\\ and\\ Local\\ Variables\\ Computation}$<br>\n<br>\nWe initialize $OUT[N]=U$ for all $N\\neq entry$, and $OUT[entry]=\\emptyset$.<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline<br>\nNode\\ (N) &amp; Instruction &amp; Eval[N] &amp; Kill[N] \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n0 &amp; entry &amp; \\emptyset &amp; \\emptyset \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n1 &amp; c=a+b &amp; \\{a+b\\} &amp; \\emptyset \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n2 &amp; f=d+e &amp; \\{d+e\\} &amp; \\emptyset \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n3 &amp; i=g+h &amp; \\{g+h\\} &amp; \\emptyset \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n4 &amp; branch &amp; \\emptyset &amp; \\emptyset \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n5 &amp; d=x*2 &amp; \\{x*2\\} &amp; \\{d+e\\}\\ (\\text{because}\\ d\\ \\text{is redefined}) \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n6 &amp; g=x*2 &amp; \\{x*2\\} &amp; \\{g+h\\}\\ (\\text{because}\\ g\\ \\text{is redefined}) \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n7 &amp; j=a+b &amp; \\{a+b\\} &amp; \\emptyset \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n8 &amp; k=d+e &amp; \\{d+e\\} &amp; \\emptyset \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n9 &amp; m=g+h &amp; \\{g+h\\} &amp; \\emptyset \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n10 &amp; exit &amp; \\emptyset &amp; \\emptyset \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{3.\\ Data\\text{-}Flow\\ Iteration\\ (Forward\\ Pass)}$<br>\n<br>\nSince this CFG is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) with no back-edges, the algorithm converges in a single pass over the topological order.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Node\\ 1:$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[1]=OUT[0]=\\emptyset<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[1]=\\{a+b\\}\\cup(\\emptyset-\\emptyset)=\\{a+b\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Node\\ 2:$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[2]=OUT[1]=\\{a+b\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[2]=\\{d+e\\}\\cup(\\{a+b\\}-\\emptyset)=\\{a+b,d+e\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Node\\ 3:$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[3]=OUT[2]=\\{a+b,d+e\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[3]=\\{g+h\\}\\cup(\\{a+b,d+e\\}-\\emptyset)=\\{a+b,d+e,g+h\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Node\\ 4:$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[4]=OUT[3]<br>\n\\Rightarrow<br>\nOUT[4]=\\{a+b,d+e,g+h\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Node\\ 5\\ (Left\\ Branch):$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[5]=OUT[4]=\\{a+b,d+e,g+h\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[5]=\\{x*2\\}\\cup(\\{a+b,d+e,g+h\\}-\\{d+e\\})<br>\n=\\{a+b,g+h,x*2\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Node\\ 6\\ (Right\\ Branch):$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[6]=OUT[4]=\\{a+b,d+e,g+h\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[6]=\\{x*2\\}\\cup(\\{a+b,d+e,g+h\\}-\\{g+h\\})<br>\n=\\{a+b,d+e,x*2\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Node\\ 7\\ (Merge\\ Point):$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[7]=OUT[5]\\cap OUT[6]<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n=\\{a+b,g+h,x*2\\}\\cap\\{a+b,d+e,x*2\\}<br>\n=\\{a+b,x*2\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[7]=\\{a+b\\}\\cup(\\{a+b,x*2\\}-\\emptyset)<br>\n=\\{a+b,x*2\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Node\\ 8:$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[8]=OUT[7]=\\{a+b,x*2\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[8]=\\{d+e\\}\\cup(\\{a+b,x*2\\}-\\emptyset)<br>\n=\\{a+b,d+e,x*2\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Node\\ 9:$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[9]=OUT[8]=\\{a+b,d+e,x*2\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[9]=\\{g+h\\}\\cup(\\{a+b,d+e,x*2\\}-\\emptyset)<br>\n=\\{a+b,d+e,g+h,x*2\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{4.\\ Evaluating\\ the\\ Options}$<br>\n<br>\nTo check if an expression evaluated at a node $N$ is redundant, we check if it is present in $IN[N]$:<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Option\\ A\\ (Node\\ 7):}$ Evaluates $a+b$. Since $a+b\\in IN[7]\\ (\\{a+b,x*2\\})$, the expression is redundant. $(\\textbf{Correct})$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Option\\ B\\ (Node\\ 8):}$ Evaluates $d+e$. Since $d+e\\notin IN[8]\\ (\\{a+b,x*2\\})$, it must be re-evaluated. It is not redundant because the path through Node $5$ killed it. $(\\textbf{Incorrect})$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Option\\ C\\ (Node\\ 9):}$ Evaluates $g+h$. Since $g+h\\notin IN[9]\\ (\\{a+b,d+e,x*2\\})$, it must be re-evaluated. It is not redundant because the path through Node $6$ killed it. $(\\textbf{Incorrect})$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Option\\ D:}$ Directly matches our computed set $IN[7]=\\{a+b,x*2\\}$. $(\\textbf{Correct})$</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532367\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":14,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following intermediate code from Exercise $9.1.4$, designed to compute the dot product of two vectors $A$ and $B$:</p><p>\\[<br>\\begin{array}{l}<br>dp\\ =\\ 0. \\\\<br>i\\ =\\ 0 \\\\<br>L:\\ t1\\ =\\ i*8 \\\\<br>\\qquad t2\\ =\\ A[t1] \\\\<br>\\qquad t3\\ =\\ i*8 \\\\<br>\\qquad t4\\ =\\ B[t3] \\\\<br>\\qquad t5\\ =\\ t2*t4 \\\\<br>\\qquad dp\\ =\\ dp+t5 \\\\<br>\\qquad i\\ =\\ i+1 \\\\<br>\\qquad if\\ i&lt;n\\ goto\\ L<br>\\end{array}<br>\\]</p><p><br><br>First, we partition this code into two standard Basic Blocks: $B_1$ (the initialization block) and $B_2$ (the loop body). We then perform an Available Expressions Analysis to identify redundant common subexpressions for optimization.</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"500\" height=\"655\" src=\"media/02/028a1eeec88fb9ba1edb.png\"></p><p><br><br>Based on the strict computation of $IN$ and $OUT$ sets, and intra-block evaluation for Available Expressions Analysis, which of the following statements are $\\textbf{CORRECT?}$ (Select all that apply)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>In block $B_2$, the expression $i*8$ computed at $t3=i*8$ is a locally redundant common subexpression.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The expression $i*8$ is globally available at the entry of block $B_2\\ (IN[B_2])$ because it is computed in the previous loop iteration.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>At the end of block $B_2\\ (OUT[B_2])$, the expression $i*8$ is $\\textbf{NOT}$ available because it is killed by a subsequent instruction in the same block.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The expression $dp+t5$ is available at $IN[B_2]$ across loop iterations.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532366\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Available Expressions Analysis is a $\\textbf{forward}$ data-flow analysis. An expression $x\\ op\\ y$ is available at a point if every path from the entry node to $p$ evaluates $x\\ op\\ y$, and after the last such evaluation prior to $p$, there are no subsequent assignments to $x$ or $y$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{1.\\ Formal\\ Data\\text{-}Flow\\ Equations}$<br>\n<br>\nLet the Universal set of arithmetic expressions be<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nU=\\{i*8,\\ t2*t4,\\ dp+t5,\\ i+1\\}.<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nLocal Sets for each block $N$:<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Eval[N]\\ (or\\ Gen[N]):$ Set of expressions evaluated in $N$ that are not subsequently killed within $N$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Kill[N]:$ Set of expressions in $U$ that have at least one operand redefined (assigned a new value) within $N$.<br>\n<br>\nGlobal Equations:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[B_1]=\\emptyset<br>\n\\qquad<br>\n(Entry\\ node)<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[N]=\\bigcap_{P\\in Pred(N)}OUT[P]<br>\n\\qquad<br>\n(For\\ N\\neq B_1)<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[N]=Eval[N]\\cup(IN[N]-Kill[N])<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{2.\\ Initialization\\ and\\ Local\\ Variables\\ Computation}$<br>\n<br>\nLets carefully compute the $Eval$ and $Kill$ for our basic blocks.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{Block\\ } B_1:$<br>\n<br>\nInstructions:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\ndp=0;\\ i=0<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Eval[B_1]=\\emptyset$<br>\n(No complex expressions are evaluated).<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ Kill[B_1]=\\{i*8,\\ i+1,\\ dp+t5\\}$<br>\n<br>\n(Because variables $i$ and $dp$ are assigned, any expression containing them is killed).<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{Block\\ } B_2:$<br>\n<br>\nInstructions:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nt1=i*8;\\ \\ldots\\ t3=i*8;\\ \\ldots\\ t5=t2*t4;\\ dp=dp+t5;\\ i=i+1;<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Intra\\text{-}block\\ Analysis\\ for\\ Redundancy:}$<br>\n<br>\nAt the instruction $t3=i*8$, the expression $i*8$ was already evaluated at $t1=i*8$. Since $i$ has $\\textbf{NOT}$ been redefined between these two lines, $i*8$ is locally redundant. (This makes Option A correct).<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Calculating\\ } Kill[B_2]:$<br>\n<br>\n$dp$ is assigned, killing $\\{dp+t5\\}$.<br>\n<br>\n$i$ is assigned, killing $\\{i*8,\\ i+1\\}$.<br>\n<br>\nSo,<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nKill[B_2]=\\{i*8,\\ i+1,\\ dp+t5\\}.<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Calculating\\ } Eval[B_2]:$<br>\n<br>\nWe only include expressions that are evaluated $\\textbf{AND}$ survive to the end of the block.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ i*8$ is evaluated, but later $i=i+1$ kills it.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ dp+t5$ is evaluated, but $dp=dp+t5$ overwrites $dp$, killing it.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ t2*t4$ is evaluated, and neither $t2$ nor $t4$ is redefined afterwards.<br>\n<br>\nThus,<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nEval[B_2]=\\{t2*t4\\}.<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{3.\\ Data\\text{-}Flow\\ Iteration\\ (Forward\\ Pass)}$<br>\n<br>\nWe initialize $OUT[N]=U$ for $N\\neq B_1$ to compute the maximum fixed point (intersection operator).<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{Initialization:}$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ OUT[B_1]=\\emptyset$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ OUT[B_2]=U=\\{i*8,\\ t2*t4,\\ dp+t5,\\ i+1\\}$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{Iteration\\ 1:}$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ B_1:$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[B_1]=\\emptyset<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[B_1]=Eval[B_1]\\cup(\\emptyset-Kill[B_1])=\\emptyset<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ B_2:$<br>\n<br>\nPredecessors of $B_2$ are $B_1$ and $B_2$ (due to the loop back-edge).<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[B_2]=OUT[B_1]\\cap OUT[B_2]<br>\n=\\emptyset\\cap U<br>\n=\\emptyset<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[B_2]=Eval[B_2]\\cup(IN[B_2]-Kill[B_2])<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n=\\{t2*t4\\}\\cup(\\emptyset-\\{i*8,i+1,dp+t5\\})<br>\n=\\{t2*t4\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{Iteration\\ 2:}$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\ B_2:$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nIN[B_2]=OUT[B_1]\\cap OUT[B_2]<br>\n=\\emptyset\\cap\\{t2*t4\\}<br>\n=\\emptyset<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nOUT[B_2]=\\{t2*t4\\}\\cup(\\emptyset-Kill[B_2])<br>\n=\\{t2*t4\\}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nThe sets have stopped changing, so the algorithm has converged.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{4.\\ Evaluating\\ the\\ Options}$<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Option\\ A\\ (Correct):}$ During our intra-block scan, $t3=i*8$ evaluates an expression already computed in the block without intervening reassignments to $i$. It is a local common subexpression.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Option\\ B\\ (Incorrect):}$ We found $IN[B_2]=\\emptyset$. The expression $i*8$ is not globally available at the start of the loop because the previous iterations $i=i+1$ instruction killed it.<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Option\\ C\\ (Correct):}$ The expression $i*8$ is not in $OUT[B_2]$ because it is in $Kill[B_2]$ (due to $i=i+1$).<br>\n<br>\n$\\bullet\\ \\textbf{Option\\ D\\ (Incorrect):}$ $IN[B_2]=\\emptyset$, so $dp+t5$ is not available. It is killed by $dp=dp+t5$ at the end of every loop.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539799\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><blockquote><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"1200\" height=\"1172\" src=\"media/4c/4cac2cd7ed0b22f50829.jpg\"></p></blockquote><p>May Helpfull&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532365\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":15,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following Control Flow Graph (CFG) consisting of 6 basic blocks ( $B_{1}$ to $B_{6}$ ) with the given intermediate Three-Address Code instructions.<br>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"500\" height=\"439\" src=\"media/5f/5f34cf65e94dc9223526.jpg\"></p><p><br><br>Perform $\\textbf{Live Variable Analysis}$ on this CFG.<br><br>What is the $\\textbf{minimum number of registers}$ required to execute this code without spilling variables to memory?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532364\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>To find the minimum number of registers required, we must perform Live Variable Analysis, determine where variables overlap (interfere) in their liveness, and then use graph coloring techniques.<br><br>$\\textbf{1.\\ Data\\text{-}Flow\\ Equations}$<br><br>Liveness Analysis is a backward data-flow analysis. For each basic block $B$:<br><br>$$<br>IN[B]=USE[B]\\cup(OUT[B]-DEF[B])<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B]=\\bigcup_{S\\in succ(B)}IN[S]<br>$$<br><br>Where:<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ USE[B]:$ Set of variables whose values are read before being written in $B$.<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ DEF[B]:$ Set of variables that are assigned a value in $B$.<br><br>$\\textbf{2.\\ Local\\ Sets\\ }(USE\\ and\\ DEF)\\ \\textbf{per\\ Block}$<br><br>$$<br>\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}<br>\\hline<br>Block &amp; Instructions &amp; USE &amp; DEF \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_1 &amp; (1)\\ a=1;\\ (2)\\ b=2 &amp; \\emptyset &amp; \\{a,b\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_2 &amp; (3)\\ c=a+b;\\ (4)\\ d=c-a &amp; \\{a,b\\} &amp; \\{c,d\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_3 &amp; (5)\\ d=b+d &amp; \\{b,d\\} &amp; \\{d\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_4 &amp; (6)\\ a=a+b;\\ (7)\\ e=e+1 &amp; \\{a,b,e\\} &amp; \\{a,e\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_5 &amp; (8)\\ b=a+b;\\ (9)\\ e=c-a &amp; \\{a,b,c\\} &amp; \\{b,e\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_6 &amp; (10)\\ m=b+d;\\ (11)\\ h=m-d &amp; \\{b,d\\} &amp; \\{m,h\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\end{array}<br>$$<br><br>$\\textbf{3.\\ Iterative\\ Liveness\\ Computation}$<br><br>We initialize $IN[B]=\\emptyset$ and $OUT[B]=\\emptyset$ for all blocks. We iterate backwards from $B_6$ up to $B_1$.<br><br>$\\textbf{Iteration\\ 1:}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_6:$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_6]=\\emptyset<br>\\Rightarrow<br>IN[B_6]=\\{b,d\\}\\cup(\\emptyset-\\{m,h\\})=\\{b,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_5:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_5)=\\{B_6\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_5]=IN[B_6]=\\{b,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_5]=\\{a,b,c\\}\\cup(\\{b,d\\}-\\{b,e\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_4:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_4)=\\{B_5\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_4]=IN[B_5]=\\{a,b,c,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_4]=\\{a,b,e\\}\\cup(\\{a,b,c,d\\}-\\{a,e\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_3:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_3)=\\{B_4,B_1\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_3]=IN[B_4]\\cup IN[B_1]<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}\\cup\\emptyset<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_3]=\\{b,d\\}\\cup(\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}-\\{d\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_2:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_2)=\\{B_3\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_2]=IN[B_3]=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_2]=\\{a,b\\}\\cup(\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}-\\{c,d\\})<br>=\\{a,b,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_1:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_1)=\\{B_2\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_1]=IN[B_2]=\\{a,b,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_1]=\\emptyset\\cup(\\{a,b,e\\}-\\{a,b\\})<br>=\\{e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\textbf{Iteration\\ 2:}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_6:$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_6]=\\emptyset<br>\\Rightarrow<br>IN[B_6]=\\{b,d\\}<br>\\qquad<br>(\\text{No change})<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_5:$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_5]=IN[B_6]=\\{b,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_5]=\\{a,b,c\\}\\cup(\\{b,d\\}-\\{b,e\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d\\}<br>\\qquad<br>(\\text{No change})<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_4:$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_4]=IN[B_5]=\\{a,b,c,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_4]=\\{a,b,e\\}\\cup(\\{a,b,c,d\\}-\\{a,e\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>\\qquad<br>(\\text{No change})<br>$$<br>To find the minimum number of registers required, we must perform Live Variable Analysis, determine where variables overlap (interfere) in their liveness, and then use graph coloring techniques.<br><br>$\\textbf{1.\\ Data\\text{-}Flow\\ Equations}$<br><br>Liveness Analysis is a backward data-flow analysis. For each basic block $B$:<br><br>$$<br>IN[B]=USE[B]\\cup(OUT[B]-DEF[B])<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B]=\\bigcup_{S\\in succ(B)}IN[S]<br>$$<br><br>Where:<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ USE[B]:$ Set of variables whose values are read before being written in $B$.<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ DEF[B]:$ Set of variables that are assigned a value in $B$.<br><br>$\\textbf{2.\\ Local\\ Sets\\ }(USE\\ and\\ DEF)\\ \\textbf{per\\ Block}$<br><br>$$<br>\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}<br>\\hline<br>Block &amp; Instructions &amp; USE &amp; DEF \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_1 &amp; (1)\\ a=1;\\ (2)\\ b=2 &amp; \\emptyset &amp; \\{a,b\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_2 &amp; (3)\\ c=a+b;\\ (4)\\ d=c-a &amp; \\{a,b\\} &amp; \\{c,d\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_3 &amp; (5)\\ d=b+d &amp; \\{b,d\\} &amp; \\{d\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_4 &amp; (6)\\ a=a+b;\\ (7)\\ e=e+1 &amp; \\{a,b,e\\} &amp; \\{a,e\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_5 &amp; (8)\\ b=a+b;\\ (9)\\ e=c-a &amp; \\{a,b,c\\} &amp; \\{b,e\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>B_6 &amp; (10)\\ m=b+d;\\ (11)\\ h=m-d &amp; \\{b,d\\} &amp; \\{m,h\\} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\end{array}<br>$$<br><br>$\\textbf{3.\\ Iterative\\ Liveness\\ Computation}$<br><br>We initialize $IN[B]=\\emptyset$ and $OUT[B]=\\emptyset$ for all blocks. We iterate backwards from $B_6$ up to $B_1$.<br><br>$\\textbf{Iteration\\ 1:}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_6:$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_6]=\\emptyset<br>\\Rightarrow<br>IN[B_6]=\\{b,d\\}\\cup(\\emptyset-\\{m,h\\})=\\{b,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_5:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_5)=\\{B_6\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_5]=IN[B_6]=\\{b,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_5]=\\{a,b,c\\}\\cup(\\{b,d\\}-\\{b,e\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_4:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_4)=\\{B_5\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_4]=IN[B_5]=\\{a,b,c,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_4]=\\{a,b,e\\}\\cup(\\{a,b,c,d\\}-\\{a,e\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_3:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_3)=\\{B_4,B_1\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_3]=IN[B_4]\\cup IN[B_1]<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}\\cup\\emptyset<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_3]=\\{b,d\\}\\cup(\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}-\\{d\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_2:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_2)=\\{B_3\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_2]=IN[B_3]=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_2]=\\{a,b\\}\\cup(\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}-\\{c,d\\})<br>=\\{a,b,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_1:$<br><br>$$<br>succ(B_1)=\\{B_2\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_1]=IN[B_2]=\\{a,b,e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_1]=\\emptyset\\cup(\\{a,b,e\\}-\\{a,b\\})<br>=\\{e\\}<br>$$<br><br>$\\textbf{Iteration\\ 2:}$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_6:$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_6]=\\emptyset<br>\\Rightarrow<br>IN[B_6]=\\{b,d\\}<br>\\qquad<br>(\\text{No change})<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_5:$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_5]=IN[B_6]=\\{b,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_5]=\\{a,b,c\\}\\cup(\\{b,d\\}-\\{b,e\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d\\}<br>\\qquad<br>(\\text{No change})<br>$$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_4:$<br><br>$$<br>OUT[B_4]=IN[B_5]=\\{a,b,c,d\\}<br>$$<br><br>$$<br>IN[B_4]=\\{a,b,e\\}\\cup(\\{a,b,c,d\\}-\\{a,e\\})<br>=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}<br>\\qquad<br>(\\text{No change})<br>$$<br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_3:\\ OUT[B_3]=IN[B_4]=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\},\\ IN[B_3]=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}\\qquad (\\text{No change})$<br><br>$\\bullet\\ \\ B_2:\\ OUT[B_2]=IN[B_3]=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\},\\ IN[B_2]=\\{a,b,e\\}\\qquad (\\text{No change})$<br><br>Iteration $3$: Calculations for $IN$ and $OUT$ stabilize. $IN[B_4]$ remains $\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}$. The algorithm has converged.<br><br>$\\textbf{4.\\ Interference\\ Graph\\ Register\\ Allocation}$<br><br>Two variables interfere if they are simultaneously live at any point in the program.<br><br>Notice the live set $IN[B_4]=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}$. This means that immediately prior to entering block $B_4$ (and exactly after block $B_3$ finishes executing line $4$), all $5$ variables $(a,b,c,d,e)$ are simultaneously live.<br><br>Because they are mutually live at the exact same execution point, every variable interferes with every other variable.<br>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"272\" src=\"media/ba/ba692bf89925181ddce1.jpg\"></p><p><br><br>The resulting graph is a Complete Graph on $5$ vertices $(K_5)$. The chromatic number of a complete graph $K_n$ is exactly $n$.<br><br>Therefore, a minimum of $5$ registers is strictly required to compile this code without spilling.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532363\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":25.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":15}],"stats":{"questions":15,"maxMarks":2,"types":{"MCQ":5,"MSQ":6,"NAT":4},"withoutKey":14}};
